from library import *
from known import KNOWN
from extras import EXTRA
K={**KNOWN,**EXTRA,'chaos':'10.1007/978-1-4757-4740-9','vectorimaging':'10.1145/166117.166151','enumerative':'10.1017/CBO9781139058520','lsystem2':'10.1016/0022-5193(68)90080-5'}
D={}
def add(slug,text,refs):D[slug]=(text,refs)
add('3d-solids', '''How can a two-dimensional display communicate three-dimensional shape? NetLogo 3D Solids samples points on selected solid surfaces, stores spherical coordinates, rotates them by changing an angle, and projects their positions into the view. Brightness supplies an additional depth cue. The graphics methods in [1], differential geometry in [2], and computational geometry in [3] provide complementary foundations for separating a surface, its parameterization, and its displayed approximation. The model illustrates coordinate conversion and rotation rather than physical motion of a rigid material. Its random point cloud is finite, the display omits a full optical rendering pipeline, and visual smoothness depends on sampling. Recognizing a convincing sphere or cube therefore demonstrates a useful representation, without proving uniform surface sampling or geometric accuracy of every generated point.''', [('graphics','Coordinate transformations, projection and depth cues explain the point-cloud representation.'),('diffgeo','Surface parameterizations distinguish spherical coordinates from the underlying geometric surface.'),('geometry','Geometric representations and spatial computations frame the distinction between sampled points and exact solids.')])
add('vector-fields', '''What does a local direction field imply about a particle's trajectory? NetLogo Vector Fields displays arrows tangent to circles around the origin, with magnitude increasing with distance, and moves particles through finite direction-following steps. Differential geometry [1] supplies the language of curves and tangent directions; computer graphics [2] explains arrows and spatial encoding; Cabral and Leedom [3] provide a related field-visualization method using line integral convolution. The original Info identifies an important numerical artifact: trajectories slowly spiral outward because particles travel before recalculating their headings. Changing step size changes this approximation. The baseline illustrates an abstract vector field rather than identifying a particular gravitational or electromagnetic law. A circular-looking vector plot alone cannot establish force-based orbital motion, since velocity fields and acceleration fields imply different differential equations.''', [('diffgeo','Curves and tangents provide the mathematical setting for integral paths.'),('graphics','Vector glyphs and spatial transformations ground the visual encoding of magnitude and direction.'),('vectorimaging','Original vector-field visualization research supplies a direct scientific comparison to NetLogo’s arrow glyphs and particle paths; NetLogo does not implement line integral convolution.')])
add('pursuit', '''How does always turning toward a moving target shape a follower's path? In NetLogo Pursuit, a leader advances along a selected mathematical function while followers repeatedly face the leader and move a fixed distance. Nahin's mathematical treatment of pursuit [1] gives the closest theoretical context. Differential geometry [2] clarifies the distinction between a target curve and the curves generated by followers, while Reynolds [3] supplies a related steering framework for autonomous motion. Step size and initial follower positions determine lag and path shape. The leader follows a prescribed, sometimes rescaled curve rather than responding strategically to pursuit. Updates are discrete and sensing is exact and instantaneous. The experiment illustrates feedback-driven trajectory construction; it does not establish interception guarantees, realistic animal pursuit, or optimal navigation under limited perception.''', [('pursuit','Mathematical pursuit is the direct conceptual antecedent of continuously aiming at a moving leader.'),('diffgeo','Curve geometry supports reasoning about the different target and follower trajectories.'),('vision','Steering toward a target is a related autonomous-agent motion mechanism; this teaching model lacks Reynolds’s full flocking rules.')])
add('3d-surface', '''How can a moving point cloud suggest a smooth surface? NetLogo 3D Surface randomly samples horizontal coordinates and computes height from a time-dependent cosine expression. It displays the resulting three-dimensional points through an oblique two-dimensional projection and varies color with height. Computer graphics [1] grounds projection and visual depth cues; differential geometry [2] explains a surface defined as a height function; computational geometry [3] frames the difference between discrete samples and an underlying continuous object. Changing sample count or the height formula reveals how representation affects perceived continuity. The displayed wave is prescribed mathematically: no elastic forces or material equations generate it. Finite sampling and projection can hide structure or introduce apparent overlaps. The simulation illustrates coordinate mapping and animated parameterization, without validating membrane mechanics or a physical wave equation.''', [('graphics','Projection and visual encoding explain the apparent depth of the sampled surface.'),('diffgeo','A height function is a surface parameterization; this is the relevant mathematical structure.'),('geometry','Discrete geometric representations clarify that the point cloud is an approximation to a continuous surface.')])
add('turtles-circling', '''What collective shape appears when independent circular motions change radius? NetLogo Turtles Circling starts agents around a common circle, then repeatedly advances and turns each agent according to the selected radius and speed. Changing the radius during motion alters each agent's local trajectory while the group traces a new aggregate pattern. Curve geometry [1] relates turning to curvature; the pursuit constructions in [2] provide comparable examples of paths generated by simple motion rules; graphics methods [3] explain coordinate and trajectory representations. The agents do not attract one another or enforce a collective boundary. Finite steps approximate the intended circular motion, and changing parameters need not preserve the original center. The model illustrates the distinction between individual paths and an emergent visible envelope, rather than a physical mechanism maintaining a ring.''', [('diffgeo','Curvature connects forward motion and turning to local circular geometry.'),('pursuit','Rule-generated paths give a mathematical comparison for repeated directional motion.'),('graphics','Coordinate transforms and curve rendering support interpretation of the aggregate traced shape.')])
add('voronoi', '''How does nearest-site assignment partition space? NetLogo Voronoi places sites and colors every grid patch according to its closest site, producing a raster approximation to a Voronoi diagram. Dragging a site changes the affected territories, which are redrawn outward from the interaction region. Fortune's algorithm [1] establishes the exact geometric construction as a useful comparison; computational geometry [2] explains diagram structure and algorithmic choices; spatial tessellation theory [3] connects nearest-site regions with scientific applications. The NetLogo baseline deliberately uses patch classification rather than calculating exact polygon vertices. Resolution, ties, finite boundaries, and coincident sites therefore matter. The experiment illustrates how a global partition follows from a local distance criterion, while its visual resemblance to cells or territories does not demonstrate any particular biological or urban growth mechanism.''', [('fortune','Exact sweepline construction contrasts with NetLogo’s raster nearest-site approximation.'),('geometry','Voronoi geometry, degeneracies and computational representations directly support the mechanism.'),('voronoi','Scientific monograph on Voronoi tessellations supplies applications and assumptions of nearest-site territorial models.')])
add('emergent-shapes-ellipse', '''Can simple feedback recover a geometric constraint without directly drawing its equation? NetLogo Emergent Shapes Ellipse asks agents to approach positions whose distances to two fixed foci sum to a prescribed constant. Agents continue when their movement improves the constraint and choose a new direction when it worsens. Differential geometry [1] gives the curve context, computational geometry [2] supplies distance-based geometric reasoning, and Rosin's ellipse-fitting study [3] provides a contrasting numerical approach with its own normalization issues. The resulting point distribution illustrates decentralized search for a level set. Agent step size, stopping tolerance, initial positions, and random choices influence the approximation. This is a demonstration of constraint satisfaction, rather than evidence that natural elliptical forms arise by this rule or that every agent samples the ellipse uniformly.''', [('diffgeo','Curve geometry provides the mathematical interpretation of the two-focus distance constraint.'),('geometry','Distance predicates and finite approximations frame the spatial constraint calculation.'),('ellipse','Ellipse fitting is a directly relevant contrasting numerical method; it does not describe NetLogo’s agent search rule.')])
add('tree-simple', '''How can repeated branching produce a tree-like form? NetLogo Tree Simple uses agents as growing branch tips; recursive reproduction and turning generate smaller copies of a basic construction. Lindenmayer's original developmental formalism [1] supplies a scientific foundation for rule-based branching, the Algorithmic Beauty of Plants [2] develops turtle interpretations of plant grammars, and Chaos and Fractals [3] explains self-similarity and finite recursive approximations. The baseline is a geometric drawing experiment, with controls for the initial location, color changes, and repeated construction. Branch resemblance depends on the chosen rule and iteration depth. It does not include carbon allocation, mechanical support, resource competition, or measured botanical parameters. The simulation illustrates how hierarchical structure can emerge from a compact generative description; resemblance to a tree is not biological validation.''', [('lsystem','Original developmental rewriting models establish the scientific context for recursive branching.'),('beauty','Turtle interpretation of branching grammars directly connects local drawing instructions with plant-like geometry.'),('chaos','Self-similarity and finite iteration explain what makes the generated tree a fractal approximation.')])
add('koch-curve', '''How can a finite replacement rule create an increasingly intricate curve? NetLogo Koch Curve repeatedly replaces each segment with four shorter segments arranged around a triangular bump. The construction makes successive approximations visible, allowing segment count, scale, and apparent roughness to be compared. Chaos and Fractals [1] explains self-similar constructions and dimension; Mandelbrot's research monograph [2] places such curves within fractal geometry; the Algorithmic Beauty of Plants [3] provides a complementary rewriting and turtle-graphics formulation. The mathematical limiting curve differs fundamentally from any finite display: the simulation always stops at a finite iteration depth and finite numerical resolution. The model illustrates recursive substitution and scaling, rather than directly simulating erosion or coastline formation. Similar-looking natural boundaries require their own mechanisms and empirical measurements before fractal analogies become explanatory claims.''', [('chaos','Self-similar replacement and fractal dimension directly explain the iterative construction.'),('mandelbrotbook','Fractal geometry provides the limiting-curve context and cautions against equating visual roughness with physical mechanism.'),('beauty','Rewriting and turtle graphics supply a scientific generative representation of recursive curves.')])
add('l-system-fractals', '''How does symbolic rewriting become geometric form? NetLogo L-System Fractals expands a string using production rules and interprets the symbols as turtle movement and turning. Repeated rewriting generates increasingly elaborate figures from short descriptions. Lindenmayer's original paper [1] establishes the developmental grammar approach, its companion paper [2] extends the treatment to branching filaments, and the Algorithmic Beauty of Plants [3] connects these formalisms to geometric turtle interpretation. The model illustrates recursion, rule composition, and the relationship between a symbolic description and its spatial realization. Chosen angles, step scales, production rules, and iteration depth determine the result. A finite rendering cannot realize an infinite fractal, and a plant-like drawing does not model physiology or establish a biological developmental explanation without additional measured assumptions.''', [('lsystem','Original production-rule approach to cellular development supplies the foundational formalism.'),('lsystem2','The companion original study develops branching filament interactions relevant to branching structures.'),('beauty','Directly develops turtle interpretation and geometric applications of L-systems.')])
if __name__=='__main__':
 for slug,(text,refs) in D.items():
  try:write(slug,text,[pub(K[k],r) for k,r in refs])
  except Exception as ex:print(slug,'ERROR',str(ex),flush=True)
